## Proof

Let me restate the problem in graph-theoretic terms. We have the complete graph $K_n$ ($n\ge 3$) whose edges are colored so that each color (VPN) forms a **connected spanning subgraph** (Full Reachability), and there is **no rainbow triangle** (No Rainbow Nodes). We want the maximum number $M$ of colors.

I claim
$$
M=\begin{cases}1,& n=3,\\[2pt]2,& n\ge 4.\end{cases}
$$

---

### Upper bound: $M\le 2$ for all $n\ge 3$

We use **Gallai's decomposition theorem** (a standard result, 1968):

> In any edge-coloring of $K_n$ ($n\ge 2$) with no rainbow triangle, there exists a partition of the vertex set into $m\ge 2$ nonempty parts $V_1,\dots,V_m$ such that
> (i) all edges between any fixed pair $V_i,V_j$ have the same color, and
> (ii) at most two colors appear on edges between different parts.

Apply this to our coloring, obtaining parts $V_1,\dots,V_m$ with $m\ge 2$, and let $S$ be the set of colors used on inter-part edges; $|S|\le 2$.

Consider any color $c\notin S$. Every edge of color $c$ lies entirely inside some single part $V_i$. Pick vertices $u\in V_1$ and $v\in V_2$ (possible since $m\ge 2$). Any path from $u$ to $v$ using only color-$c$ edges would have to cross from one part to another at some step, but no color-$c$ edge crosses between parts. Hence $u$ and $v$ lie in different components of the color-$c$ subgraph, so color $c$ is **not** connected and spanning.

Therefore every VPN color must belong to $S$, giving
$$
M\le |S|\le 2. \tag{1}
$$

For $n=3$ we can sharpen this: $K_3$ has only $3$ edges, while a connected spanning subgraph on $3$ vertices needs at least $2$ edges, so two VPNs would need $\ge 4$ edges — impossible. Thus
$$
M(3)\le 1. \tag{2}
$$

---

### Lower bound: $M\ge 2$ for $n\ge 4$

Take the Hamiltonian path $P=1\!-\!2\!-\!3\!-\!\cdots\!-\!n$ and use **two** colors:
- **Color 1**: all edges of $P$ (a spanning tree, hence connected and spanning).
- **Color 2**: every other edge of $K_n$ (the complement of $P$).

**No rainbow triangle:** with only two colors present, any triangle's three edges are colored by two colors, so by the pigeonhole principle at least two edges share a color. Hence no triangle is rainbow. ✓

**Color 1 is connected spanning:** $P$ is a spanning tree. ✓

**Color 2 is connected spanning:** The complement $H$ of $P$ contains every pair $\{i,j\}$ with $|i-j|\ne 1$. Vertex $1$ is adjacent in $H$ to $3,4,\dots,n$ (all except $2$), so $1$ reaches every vertex except possibly $2$. Vertex $2$ is adjacent in $H$ to $4,5,\dots,n$ (all except $1$ and $3$); since $n\ge 4$, vertex $4$ exists, and $4$ is adjacent to $1$ in $H$. Thus $2\!-\!4\!-\!1$ is a path in $H$, connecting $2$ to the rest. So $H$ is connected and spanning. ✓

Therefore $M(n)\ge 2$ for every $n\ge 4$. Together with (1),
$$
M(n)=2\quad\text{for }n\ge 4. \tag{3}
$$

For $n=3$, using a single color on all three edges gives a connected spanning VPN with no rainbow triangle, so $M(3)\ge 1$. Together with (2),
$$
M(3)=1. \tag{4}
$$

---

### Conclusion

Combining (3) and (4),

$$
\boxed{M=\begin{cases}1,& n=3,\\[2pt]2,& n\ge 4.\end{cases}}
$$

